# A closure exists to compute a value, so its body should parse in expression mode
# regardless of where the closure literal itself is written -- not only when the
# literal happens to be wrapped in an extra pair of parentheses. Currently a closure
# typed directly in command-mode context (e.g. the right-hand side of `:=` at the top
# level of a script, which starts in command mode) still gets a command-mode body,
# forcing every expression inside it to be individually wrapped in `()`
# (`{|$x| ($x + 1)}` instead of the more natural `{|$x| $x + 1}`). The same closure
# defined inside an extra pair of parens, e.g. `$f := ({|$x| $x + 1})`, already works
# today; this test is about the literal typed without that workaround.

$add_one := {|$x| $x + 1}
assert (($add_one(5)) == 6) "a closure defined directly at the top level should parse its body in expression mode"

$greater := {|$a $b| $a > $b}
assert ($greater(5, 3)) "operators other than + should also work directly in a top-level closure body"
